WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:03.646 --> 00:00:11.500
You've already seen the most important components in a RAG system, namely the LLM itself, a knowledge base of relevant information, and a retriever that can search the knowledge base.

2
00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:17.500
Now let's look at the overall architecture of a RAG system and how these components work together.

3
00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:21.500
Let's start by looking at how you normally use a large language model.

4
00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:24.500
You type a prompt and send it to the LLM.

5
00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:28.500
The LLM processes the prompt and generates a response.

6
00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:32.500
The user experience for a RAG system is actually identical.

7
00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:35.500
You submit a prompt and get back a response.

8
00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:39.500
Inside the system, however, there's a few more steps.

9
00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:44.500
When the RAG system receives your prompt, it first routes it to the retriever.

10
00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:51.500
The retriever has access to the knowledge base, which, practically speaking, is just a database of useful documents.

11
00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:58.500
The retriever queries the database, returning documents that it determines are most relevant to the prompt.

12
00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:07.000
Next, the system creates an augmented prompt, incorporating information from the relevant documents into the original prompt.

13
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:10.000
For example, an augmented prompt might be,

14
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:12.000
Answer the following question.

15
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:16.000
Why are hotels in Vancouver so expensive this coming weekend?

16
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:19.000
Here are five relevant articles that may help you respond.

17
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:22.000
And then insert text from the articles.

18
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:26.000
At this point, the system operates like any other LLM.

19
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:30.500
The augmented prompt is sent to the LLM and it generates a response.

20
00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:36.500
The LLM is able to respond based on both the knowledge it learned from its training data,

21
00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:41.500
as well as any additional context provided by the retrieved documents.

22
00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:47.000
The user experience is the same as always, perhaps with a little bit of added latency.

23
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:50.000
You type a prompt and you get back a response.

24
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:54.000
Thanks to that side route through the retriever, however,

25
00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:59.000
there's a higher likelihood the response is accurate, up to date, and context aware.

26
00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:05.000
As you just saw, the main difference between using an LLM directly and a RAG system

27
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:07.500
is the addition of the retriever.

28
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:12.500
This fairly straightforward addition, however, allows for a number of advantages.

29
00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:19.500
First and most importantly, it makes information available to the LLM that might otherwise not be.

30
00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:25.500
Whether it's company policies, a piece of personal information, or this morning's headlines,

31
00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:30.500
RAG is often the only way to make some kinds of information available to an LLM.

32
00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:37.000
Related to this first point, RAG reduces the likelihood of hallucinations or misleading responses.

33
00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:41.000
These are often caused by LLMs generating responses about topics

34
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:45.500
which were excluded from their training data, or perhaps just mentioned rarely.

35
00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:51.000
Adding relevant information directly in the prompt grounds the language model's responses

36
00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:55.000
and makes them less likely to create generic or misleading text.

37
00:02:55.000 --> 00:03:00.500
RAG makes it much easier to keep LLMs up to date with rapidly changing information.

38
00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:05.500
Retraining a language model is usually a costly and time-consuming feat,

39
00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:09.500
so LLMs struggle to keep up with very new information.

40
00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:14.500
In a RAG system, however, you can simply update the information in the knowledge base,

41
00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:18.000
just like you would update entries in any other database.

42
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:24.000
As soon as those changes are indexed, your LLM will be able to respond based on the new information.

43
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:27.000
RAG improves an LLM's ability to cite sources.

44
00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:31.000
A RAG system can add citation information to the augmented prompt,

45
00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:36.000
and the LLM can then include that information in its ultimate response.

46
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:38.000
This doesn't just ground the response,

47
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:42.500
but enables human readers to dig deeper and validate the generated text.

48
00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:47.000
Finally, RAG allows an LLM to focus on this text generation.

49
00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:50.500
The retriever filters down a vast world of information,

50
00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:56.000
finds what's most important and relevant, and presents it succinctly.

51
00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:59.000
The LLM still needs to write a good response,

52
00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:03.500
but it's not being relied upon to do fact-finding or filtering steps.

53
00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:09.500
In other words, each component is assigned to work on the area of its greatest strength.

54
00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:14.500
By the end of this course, you'll dive deep on building RAG systems from the ground up,

55
00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:18.000
but here's a very simple code demo of how RAG works,

56
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:21.000
with most of the details abstracted away.

57
00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:25.000
I have a retrieve function and a generate function.

58
00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:29.000
The retrieve function is a wrapper around a retriever.

59
00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:35.000
It accepts a text query and returns relevant documents from the knowledge base.

60
00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:40.500
The generate function is just a wrapper around a large language model.

61
00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:45.000
It accepts a text prompt and returns the LLM's response.

62
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:48.500
I'll write a prompt and save it in the variable prompt.

63
00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:54.000
The prompt is, why are hotel prices in Vancouver super expensive this weekend?

64
00:04:54.000 --> 00:05:01.000
Now, let's submit our prompt directly to our LLM and see what answer it returns.

65
00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:09.000
Okay, now let's see what additional information our retriever has about this prompt, like this.

66
00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:14.000
And now let's build an augmented prompt that contains both the original user question

67
00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:16.000
and the retrieved information.

68
00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:20.000
So here, the augmented prompt reads, respond to the following prompt.

69
00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:25.500
We add in our prompt, using the following information retrieved to help you answer.

70
00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:28.000
And then we add in our retrieved documents.

71
00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:32.000
Now, let's submit this augmented prompt to our LLM.

72
00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:35.500
Now the LLM is able to provide an accurate response to the question,

73
00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:39.000
integrating the information that was retrieved from the knowledge base.

74
00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:41.500
That's really all there is to RAG.

75
00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:47.000
Adding additional context to your prompt to help an LLM respond more accurately.

76
00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:51.500
This was admittedly a very high level overview of a RAG architecture.

77
00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:56.500
So join me in the next video and we'll start looking at each component in more detail.

